message.content:
Fix three issues in the Zip Code Checker Shopify app at /Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Shopify App/Zip-Code.

---

## ISSUE 1: Widget page live preview must be sticky

File: `app/routes/app.widget.tsx`

Read this file completely first. The page uses a two-column Polaris `Layout` — the left column has all the configuration sections (Add widget to storefront, Layout, Colors, Text, etc.), the right column has the "Live Preview" card.

**Fix:** Make the right-column Live Preview card sticky so it stays in view while the user scrolls the left column.

In Polaris apps embedded in Shopify admin (iframe), you can achieve this with a `position: sticky; top: var(--p-space-400)` style on the wrapping element. Polaris Layout.Section doesn't take a `style` prop directly, so wrap the Live Preview `Card` in a `<div style={{ position: "sticky", top: "var(--p-space-400)" }}>` and apply `overflow: visible` if needed.

The exact change: find the `<Layout.Section variant="oneThird">` (or similar) that wraps the Live Preview card, and wrap its contents in:
```tsx
<div style={{ position: "sticky", top: "var(--p-space-400)" }}>
  {/* existing Live Preview card */}
</div>
```

---

## ISSUE 2: Add sample CSV download inside the import modal

File: `app/routes/app.zip-codes.tsx`

The import CSV modal already exists. Inside it, below the DropZone / file upload area, add a download link for a sample CSV so merchants know the exact format.

1. Create a constant for the sample CSV content. The existing CSV format uses: `Zip Code,Zone,Status,Message,ETA` — confirm this matches the actual import parser (read the `csv-import` action handler).

2. Inside the import modal (in the Modal.Section), BELOW the DropZone, add:
```tsx
<InlineStack align="center">
  <Button
    variant="plain"
    icon={ExportIcon}
    onClick={() => {
      const sample = "Zip Code,Zone,Status,Message,ETA\n10001,Manhattan,allowed,We deliver to your area!,2-3 business days\n10002,Manhattan,allowed,Same-day delivery available,Same day\n90210,LA,blocked,Sorry we do not deliver here yet,";
      const blob = new Blob([sample], { type: "text/csv" });
      const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      const a = document.createElement("a");
      a.href = url;
      a.download = "sample-zip-codes.csv";
      a.click();
      URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
    }}
  >
    Download sample CSV
  </Button>
</InlineStack>
```

Make sure ExportIcon is imported from `@shopify/polaris-icons` if not already.

---

## ISSUE 3: Fix duplicate export downloads

File: `app/routes/app.zip-codes.tsx`

The CSV export is currently triggering multiple download requests. Read the export handler code (look for `intent === "export"` or the export action, and also the UI button that triggers it).

Common causes:
1. **Multiple fetcher submissions**: The export button may be inside a form that submits via both the fetcher AND a native form submit. Check if there's a `<Form>` wrapping it that shouldn't be.
2. **Effect firing multiple times**: If there's a `useEffect` that watches `fetcher.data` and triggers a download, it may fire on every render. Ensure it has proper dependencies and a cleanup mechanism.
3. **Button click handler calling fetcher.submit AND native form submit**: Look for dual triggers.

The typical fix is: add a `useRef` to track whether a download has already been initiated for the current export result, so the effect only fires once per export:

```typescript
const exportHandled = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (
    fetcher.state === "idle" &&
    fetcher.data &&
    "csv" in fetcher.data &&
    !exportHandled.current
  ) {
    exportHandled.current = true;
    const blob = new Blob([fetcher.data.csv as string], { type: "text/csv" });
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const a = document.createElement("a");
    a.href = url;
    a.download = "zip-codes.csv";
    a.click();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  }
  if (fetcher.state === "submitting") {
    exportHandled.current = false; // reset for next export
  }
}, [fetcher.state, fetcher.data]);
```

Read the actual export code first to understand what pattern is used, then apply the correct fix.

---

After all fixes: run `npm run typecheck` and fix ALL TypeScript errors. Run `npm run lint` and fix ALL lint errors.

